om: 14px;"><strong>Unit 3 synthesis:</strong> Radical and rational relationships.</p><p style="margin-bottom: 14px;">In student language: <strong>I can decide which Unit 3 strategy is needed and explain why my answer is valid.</strong></p>')
P02=(Top "08" "P02 Notebook Task Part 1")+(Card "#fffbeb" "#f59e0b" "Notebook Setup" '<p style="margin-bottom: 14px;">Title your notes: <strong>Lesson 8: Putting It All Together</strong>.</p><p style="margin-bottom: 14px;">Write the standard: <strong>Unit 3 standards synthesis</strong>.</p>')+(Card "#f8fafc" "#64748b" "Key Vocabulary Review" '<ul style="padding-left: 26px; margin-bottom: 16px;"><li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Radical expression, radical function, radical equation, and extraneous solution.</li><li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Rational expression, restriction, rational equation, vertical asymptote, and horizontal asymptote.</li><li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Context validity: whether a solution makes sense in a real situation.</li></ul>')+(Card "#ecfdf5" "#059669" "Correct Example" '<div style="background: #dcfce7; border: 2px solid #16a34a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px;"><strong>Correct:</strong> For &radic;(x + 4) = 6, x = 32 because x + 4 = 36 and the check works.</div>')+(Card "#fef2f2" "#dc2626" "Incorrect Example" '<div style="background: #fee2e2; border: 2px solid #dc2626; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px;"><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Reporting x = 0 for a rational equation with denominator x is invalid because x = 0 is restricted.</div>')
P03=(Top "08" "P03 Notebook Task Part 2")+(Card "#fffbeb" "#f59e0b" "Notebook Task" '<ol style="padding-left: 26px; margin-bottom: 16px;"><li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Create a two-column review chart: radical strategies and rational strategies.</li><li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Include one example that requires checking for extraneous solutions.</li><li style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Include one example that requires restrictions from a denominator.</li></ol>')+(Card "#ecfdf5" "#059669" "Correct Reasoning" '<div style="background: #dcfce7; border: 2px solid #16a34a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px;"><strong>Correct:</strong> Radical equations require checking after squaring; rational equations require restrictions before clearing denominators.</div>')+(Card "#fef2f2" "#dc2626" "Common Mistake" '<div style="background: #fee2e2; border: 2px solid #dc2626; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px;"><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Treating every algebraic candidate as valid is incorrect because Unit 3 problems often include restrictions.</div>')
P04=(Top "08" "P04 Concept Review / Worked Examples")+(Card "#f8fafc" "#64748b" "Concept Review" '<p style="margin-bottom: 14px;">Unit 3 connects equivalent forms, functions, equations, and context. Match the problem type to the strategy: simplify expressions, analyze graphs, solve equations, or interpret models.</p>')+(Card "#ecfdf5" "#059669" "Worked Examples" '<p style="margin-bottom: 14px;"><strong>Example 1:</strong> 81<sup>3/4</sup> = 27 because the fourth root of 81 is 3 and 3<sup>3</sup> = 27.</p><p style="margin-bottom: 14px;"><strong>Example 2:</strong> &radic;(x - 1) = 4 gives x = 17 and checks.</p><p style="margin-bottom: 14px;"><strong>Example 3:</strong> (x<sup>2</sup> - 9)/(x - 3) = x + 3, with x &ne; 3.</p><p style="margin-bottom: 14px;"><strong>Example 4:</strong> f(x) = 2/(x + 5) has vertical asymptote x = -5.</p>')+(Card "#ecfdf5" "#059669" "Correct Reasoning Check" '<div style="background: #dcfce7; border: 2px solid #16a34a; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px;"><strong>Correct:</strong> A final answer is complete only when restrictions and context validity have been checked.</div>')+(Card "#fef2f2" "#dc2626" "Common Mistake" '<div style="background: #fee2e2; border: 2px solid #dc2626; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px;"><strong>Incorrect:</strong> Canceling terms instead of factors in a rational expression leads to non-equivalent expressions.</div>')
